From: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] AP MLD: fix FT roaming between AP MLDs (RRB self-learning, per-link FT check)

Two problems showed up when a non-AP MLD roamed with FT between two AP
MLDs whose affiliated APs all share the AP MLD MAC address as R1KH-ID
and use the wildcard r0kh/r1kh configuration:

1. RRB frames from a sibling link were processed as if they came from a
   remote key holder. Every affiliated AP of an AP MLD has its own l2
   socket on the FT interface, so a broadcast PMK-R1 pull or sequence
   request sent by one link is also received by the other links of the
   same AP MLD. Because those links carry the same R1KH-ID, the sequence
   request handler classified the frame as "local R1KH-ID, remote
   R0KH-ID" and answered it, which taught the requesting link that the
   remote R0KH-ID lives at its sibling's address. All later pull
   requests for that R0KH-ID were then sent to the sibling link ("R0KH-ID
   mismatch") and, after the retries, the R0KH-ID was blocked: the roam
   failed and the non-AP MLD gave up on the target AP MLD. Ignore RRB
   frames whose source address is an affiliated AP of our own AP MLD.

2. The FT Reassociation Request validation ran again for every
   affiliated link in ieee80211_ml_process_link(), with the per-STA
   profile elements only (no RSNE, MDE or FTE, as those are inherited),
   so every link other than the association link was rejected with "No
   RSNIE in Reassoc Req" and the non-AP MLD ended up single-link after
   the roam. The FT validation covers the whole MLD and has already
   been done for the association link with the same RSN state machine,
   so skip it when processing the other links.

Signed-off-by: Kamil Bienkiewicz <perceivalpercy@gmail.com>
---
--- a/src/ap/wpa_auth_glue.c
+++ b/src/ap/wpa_auth_glue.c
@@ -1588,6 +1588,32 @@
 }
 
 
+/*
+ * Every affiliated AP of an AP MLD receives the RRB frames that its sibling
+ * links send on the shared FT interface (broadcast pull and sequence requests
+ * with the wildcard configuration). They share the R1KH-ID, so such a frame
+ * would be taken for a request from a remote key holder and answered, which
+ * makes the sending link learn its own AP MLD as the address of the remote
+ * R0KH. Ignore anything that originates from our own AP MLD.
+ */
+static bool hostapd_rrb_from_own_mld(struct hostapd_data *hapd,
+				     const u8 *src_addr)
+{
+#ifdef CONFIG_IEEE80211BE
+	struct hostapd_data *link;
+
+	if (!hapd->conf->mld_ap || !hapd->mld)
+		return false;
+
+	for_each_mld_link(link, hapd) {
+		if (ether_addr_equal(link->own_addr, src_addr))
+			return true;
+	}
+#endif /* CONFIG_IEEE80211BE */
+	return false;
+}
+
+
 static void hostapd_rrb_receive(void *ctx, const u8 *src_addr, const u8 *buf,
 				size_t len)
 {
@@ -1601,6 +1627,8 @@
 	if (!is_multicast_ether_addr(ethhdr->h_dest) &&
 	    !ether_addr_equal(hapd->own_addr, ethhdr->h_dest))
 		return;
+	if (hostapd_rrb_from_own_mld(hapd, ethhdr->h_source))
+		return;
 	wpa_ft_rrb_rx(hapd->wpa_auth, ethhdr->h_source, buf + sizeof(*ethhdr),
 		      len - sizeof(*ethhdr));
 }
@@ -1617,6 +1645,8 @@
 	if (!is_multicast_ether_addr(dst_addr) &&
 	    !ether_addr_equal(hapd->own_addr, dst_addr))
 		return;
+	if (hostapd_rrb_from_own_mld(hapd, src_addr))
+		return;
 	wpa_ft_rrb_oui_rx(hapd->wpa_auth, src_addr, dst_addr, oui_suffix, buf,
 			  len);
 }
--- a/src/ap/ieee802_11.c
+++ b/src/ap/ieee802_11.c
@@ -5749,7 +5749,13 @@
 #endif /* CONFIG_PMKSA_PRIVACY */
 
 #ifdef CONFIG_IEEE80211R_AP
-		if (sta->auth_alg == WLAN_AUTH_FT) {
+		/*
+		 * The FT validation covers the whole non-AP MLD and was done
+		 * for the association link; the per-STA profiles of the other
+		 * links do not repeat the inherited RSNE, MDE and FTE, so do
+		 * not run it again when processing those links.
+		 */
+		if (sta->auth_alg == WLAN_AUTH_FT && !assoc_wpa_sm) {
 			if (type != LINK_PARSE_REASSOC) {
 				wpa_printf(MSG_DEBUG, "FT: " MACSTR " tried "
 					   "to use association (not "
